<article class="env-lemma" id="00R2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00R2">Lemma <span data-tag="00R2">10.6.3</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map of finite presentation. For any surjection $\alpha : R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n] \to S$ the kernel of $\alpha $ is a finitely generated ideal in $R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Write $S = R[y_1, \ldots , y_ m]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ k)$. Choose $g_ i \in R[y_1, \ldots , y_ m]$ which are lifts of $\alpha (x_ i)$. Then we see that $S = R[x_ i, y_ j]/(f_ l, x_ i - g_ i)$. Choose $h_ j \in R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ such that $\alpha (h_ j)$ corresponds to $y_ j \bmod (f_1, \ldots , f_ k)$. Consider the map $\psi : R[x_ i, y_ j] \to R[x_ i]$, $x_ i \mapsto x_ i$, $y_ j \mapsto h_ j$. Then the kernel of $\alpha $ is the image of $(f_ l, x_ i - g_ i)$ under $\psi $ and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>